"The surah begins with the command \"Say.\" The Prophet (peace be upon him) was commanded to say \"Say.\" This command is addressed not only to the Prophet, but to all believers until the Day of Resurrection. For this reason, the surahs were recorded verbatim in the copies of the Quran (mushafs) and reciting them in this manner became obligatory (wajib). As Imam al-Maturidi also stated, the one charged with this command is not solely the direct addressee, but every accountable individual. Whoever recites it does so as though commanding both himself and everyone else to utter this supplication.\n\nThe word falaq derives from the root falq, meaning 'to split' or 'to cleave.' In the lexicon, it bears numerous meanings, such as a fissure, a crack, the light of dawn, creatures, or a pit in Hell. Its most comprehensive meaning encompasses all creatures that have been cleaved out of nonexistence. In this sense, falaq stands in contrast to the divine attribute of al-Samad. That is to say, falaq denotes everything that is created and destined to perish. As for the meaning of daybreak, it symbolizes the light that emerges after darkness and the relief that follows distress. In this respect, the expression 'Rabbi'l-falaq' bestows a promise of deliverance and hope upon the one seeking refuge.\n\nThe use of the name al-Rabb (the Lord) here indicates that the one seeking refuge is in need of the Lord in every state. The servant can never be independent of the Lord in any matter. Furthermore, saying 'the Lord of falaq' signifies that He is the Lord of both the one sought refuge in and the one seeking refuge. This emphasizes the need for protection against both external and internal evils. As Ibn Sina also pointed out, there is a subtle secret here: the servant is commanded to seek refuge in his Lord, which serves as a reminder that perfection and good come from Allah, whereas evil stems from one's own lower self.\n\nIn the second verse, by stating, 'From the evil of what He has created,' it is commanded to seek refuge in Allah from every kind of evil that He has created. This evil encompasses every sort of harm, whether material or spiritual, worldly or otherworldly, originating from outside or arising from one's own soul. All kinds of creatures and phenomena—such as humans, jinn, animals, insects, microbes, fire, and poison—as well as the evil of sins, desires (hawa), the lower self (nafs), and corrupt deeds, fall within this scope. All evils directed against the one seeking refuge are covered by this verse. The evil of the self is also included, for the greatest enemy lies between a person's two sides.\n\nIn the third verse, it is stated, 'And from the evil of ghasiq when it enters/overtakes (waqaba).' Ghasiq carries meanings such as the night, the moon, the Pleiades (Thurayya), a snake, or the groin. The most renowned interpretation is the darkness of the night. Waqab, on the other hand, means to enter, descend, fall, or set. Accordingly, the verse means, 'from the evil of the night when its darkness settles.' Evils that occur at night and sudden afflictions fall under this evil. The eclipse or setting of the moon has also been interpreted within this scope. This verse teaches seeking protection especially against perils that emerge during dark and hidden times.\n\nAccording to a view cited by al-Razi, Surah al-Falaq and Surah al-Nas explain the ranks of created beings. Evil occurs in the corporeal realm. Bodies are either ethereal (subtle, orderly) or elemental (dense, corruptible). There is no disorder in ethereal bodies; they are entirely good. Elemental bodies, on the other hand, can be inanimate, plant, or animal. In inanimate objects, there is no appetitive faculty; utter darkness prevails. This is precisely what the expression 'from the evil of the night when it darkens' points to. Plants possess the faculty of growth. Animals possess faculties such as sensation, lust, and anger, and these deter the human soul from being devoted to Allah. The verse 'and from the evil of an envier when he envies' also depicts this animalistic faculty. After this level remains the human soul (nafs), whose degrees are mentioned in Surah al-Nas.\n\nThe word 'ghasiq' has been interpreted in various ways. While al-Razi specifies it as inanimate bodies (pure darkness), Ibn Sina interpreted it as the animalistic faculty (a turbid dark power). In our view, in accordance with the progression of the verses from the general to the specific, it is more accurate to understand ghasiq as elemental bodies in an absolute sense (including plants and animals), and then to specify the vegetative faculty followed by the animalistic faculty. In this case, the evil of ghasiq encompasses all the evils of the material realm in its state of generation and corruption; it points to the scourge of every kind of dark force or phantom confronting human beings, and even to the grim end of the mortal body condemned to enter the grave.\n\nAccording to another interpretation, ghasiq refers to every sort of dark calamity that strikes a person; material or spiritual evil, grief, and sorrow are described as darkness. In this case, 'waqub' means to assault or attack. Ibn Jarir, on the other hand, conveys the following view: Allah Almighty commanded His Prophet to seek refuge from the evil of ghasiq. Ghasiq is that which is dark; when the night grows dark, when a star sets, or when the moon is eclipsed, it is called ghasiq. Allah did not single out any of these, but generalized it. Therefore, refuge is sought from the evil of anything that can be called ghasiq at the moment of its occurrence.\n\nThe phrase 'naffathati fi'l-ukad' in the fourth verse means 'those who blow onto knots.' 'Nafth' (to blow) means blowing with a slight amount of saliva or without saliva at all. The author of al-Kashshaf states that it involves blowing with saliva, whereas the author of al-Lawami' notes that it may also be without saliva. Al-Raghib states that nafth is casting out a little spit and that it is less than spitting. Sorcerers use their vile breaths when performing magic. In contrast to this, there is also ruqyah (reciting and blowing) performed for good; beautiful words such as sermons, admonitions, and spiritual healing fall within this category. However, what is commanded to be sought refuge from here is the vile, venomous blowings of male and female sorcerers.\n\nThe word 'ukad' is the plural of 'ukdah.' Numerous meanings are enumerated in al-Qamus: a knot, a knotted place; guardianship over cities; a covenant concluded for governors; property; a densely wooded and tangled place; sufficient pasture; that upon which a person's livelihood depends; fertile land; an animal forced to browse on trees; binding obligation (such as the bond of marriage or a sales contract); a belief, resolve, or intention in the heart; and malice and anger. Al-Raghib notes that ukdah is the name of that which is tied or concluded, and that it is also used for marriage, oaths, and an impediment of speech. The sorcerer's knots are the symbols of his demonic resolves and intentions. 'Azimah' (firm resolve) is the heart's resolute commitment to a matter, and the sorcerer's resolve is directed toward evil and deviltry.\n\nThe occurrence of 'naffathat' in the feminine plural form is interpreted in three ways: First, with the implied noun being 'nisa'' (women), it means 'the blowing women/witches,' because witchcraft is predominantly the craft of women, and the spellbinding effect of female guile is potent. Second, with the implied noun being 'nufus' (souls), it means 'the blowing souls,' which is the most preferred opinion since it encompasses both male and female. Third, with the implied noun being 'jama'at' (groups/communities), it means 'the blowing groups,' as spells performed collectively by sorcerers are more potent.\n\nIn the books of tafsir, the primary meaning given to the verse is: From the evil of the sorceresses (or souls, or groups) who practice incantations and charms by tying and blowing onto knots of thread. Reports from Companions and Successors such as Ibn Abbas, al-Hasan, Qatadah, Mujahid, and Ikrimah are to this effect. Ruqyahs that are free from sorcery and performed solely to seek refuge in Allah and ask for healing are permissible. The prohibition of ruqyah in the hadiths applies to those carrying the potential of polytheism (shirk) and sorcery. In conclusion, this verse commands seeking refuge in Allah from evil breaths of the kind associated with witchcraft and magic.\n\nIn this section, first of all, the subject of warning against satanic incantations and succumbing to delusions is addressed. Most commentators have concentrated the meaning here on sorcery and stated that one must particularly beware of incantations (ruqyah) mixed with magic. They considered reciting while tying knots in thread to be a type of sorcery; indeed, some scholars such as Ikrimah even viewed blowing and stroking with the hand as suspect. However, what is meant here are spit-filled blowings and smearings that shatter the nerves; otherwise, the existence of sacred and divine (rahmani) blowings is not denied. In accordance with the hadith that there is a cure for everything, just as treatment for spiritual illnesses through spiritual means and for physical illnesses through physical means is lawful, mixed treatment is likewise lawful for mixed conditions. On condition that the effect is known to come not from the means, but from Allah, and that all kinds of intrigue, sorcery, charlatanry, deception, and causing harm are avoided.\n\nThere are many hadiths demonstrating the legitimacy of ruqyah (recitation and blowing). Among the hadiths recorded by al-Razi, there are examples such as Gabriel (Jibril) reciting over the Messenger of Allah, the prayers of seeking refuge recited over Hasan and Husayn, and Uthman ibn Abi al-As reciting over himself. Although there is no indication of blowing in these, the commands in the Quran for supplication and seeking refuge, together with these surahs, are sufficient. It is also established that every night the Messenger of Allah recited the Mu'awwidhat, blew into his hands, and wiped his face and body. While these narrations show that ruqyah is permissible, it is emphasized that the requirement of true piety is for a person to pray directly to Allah and not to seek the mediation of another.\n\nIn the principles of Islamic jurisprudence (usul al-fiqh), treatment and ruqyah are divided into three categories: definitive (maqtu'), probable (maznun), and illusory (mawhum). Ruqyah is considered part of the illusory category. According to Hanafi jurisprudence, treatment is permissible on condition that healing is recognized as coming from Allah; however, in those that are illusory like ruqyah, abandoning them is more in keeping with reliance upon Allah (tawakkul). It is forbidden (haram) to abandon definitive treatments, permissible to practice those based on probable conjecture, and more virtuous to abandon those based on illusion. This distinction does not change with the advancement of medicine, because in every era there exist degrees of certainty, presumption, and illusion. Indeed, even in modern medicine, one-hundred-percent certainty cannot be claimed.\n\nThe expression \"the women who blow on knots\" (naffathat fi al-'uqad) has been interpreted in different ways. According to one view, these are sorceresses or women who lead men into temptation (fitnah). Another view, favored by Ibn Sina and some gnomic scholars (arifs), is that this points to powers in fundamental bodies, such as the vegetative soul and the power of growth. According to this interpretation, certain faculties in the human body, such as the power of lust, act like blowing on knots. Furthermore, it has been stated that the word 'uqdah (knot) can also be used for spiritual and legal ties, such as the pledge of allegiance (bay'ah) or guardianship (wilayah). However, the general consensus is that this expression means seeking refuge from the evil of male and female sorcerers.\n\nThe definition of envy (hasad) and seeking refuge from its evil are addressed in the commentary on the fifth verse of the surah. Envy is wishing for a blessing to disappear from its possessor. While the believer feels harmless emulation (ghibtah), the hypocrite envies. When an envier envies, he brings forth his evil; at this time, evil looks or physical assaults may occur. For this reason, seeking refuge in Allah is commanded. Envy is often mentioned together with the evil eye, and the soul of the envier takes on an ugly state at that moment. All kinds of trickery may be resorted to in order to harm the envied person. However, as long as envy remains in thought, it causes no harm; but when it is put into action, one must protect oneself from its evil.\n\nEnvy means wishing for a blessing possessed by another to disappear. In addition, wishing for a deficiency in another to persist is also called envy. The common meaning is the first one. The envier is in an unloved state in the sight of Allah and His servants, and it has been counted among the major sins. However, if one does not act upon the innate feeling of envy—that is, the disposition stemming from creation—and struggles against one's ego (nafs) and treats one's brother well, it is not a sin; in fact, one earns a reward. Ghibtah, on the other hand, is wishing for the same blessing to be in oneself without wishing for it to disappear from the other, and there is no harm in this.\n\nEnvy should not be confused with jealousy. Jealousy is mostly expressed in Arabic as 'ghayrah' and carries the purpose of protecting values such as honor and dignity. This type of jealousy is praised. However, being unable to tolerate another's spouse, wealth, beauty, or any blessing, and wishing for its destruction, is envy and is condemned. In this case, the word 'jealousy' is used metaphorically. True jealousy is protective zeal (ghayrah) and sense of honor (hamiyyah), and as long as it is not taken to excess, it is commendable.\n\nEnvy is at the same time a form of injustice (dhulm). Wishing for a blessing to disappear from its rightful owner is envy. However, wishing for a blessing in the hands of an usurper to disappear or striving to deliver what is due to its owner is not envy, but zeal and justice. As long as the feeling of envy remains inside, it harms only the envier and touches no one else. But if this feeling is translated into action and an attempt is made to destroy the blessing, then it becomes injustice and transgression, and one must seek refuge in Allah. Ibn Sina states that the evil of the envier stems from the struggle between body and soul.\n\nIn this surah, by enumerating the evils that could harm the human soul, seeking refuge in Allah is commanded. First, by saying \"from the evil of what He created,\" the origin of all evils is briefly mentioned. Then, the dark night, the blowers (incantation-makers), and enviers are specifically mentioned. These three explain the most important evils or their ranks. At the end of the surah, by specifically mentioning the evil of the envier, the protection of the human soul against evils that may come from outside is aimed at.\n\nThe words 'ghasiq' and 'hasid' occurring in the surah are brought in the indefinite (nakirah) and singular form, whereas 'naffathat' is brought in the definite (ma'rifah) and plural form. Some scholars have stated that not every ghasiq and hasid is evil, but every blower is evil. In our view, while the evil of the ghasiq and the hasid generally occurs in secret and in darkness, the blowers commit evil more openly. For this reason, 'naffathat' appears as definite and plural. This surah, being completed by Surah an-Nas, teaches seeking refuge in Allah from evils coming from both outside and inside."